  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Tacoma &amp; Everett Smelter Soil Plumes</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Understanding historical airborne smelter emissions across King and Snohomish counties: direct links to official state DirtAlert maps, free Department of Ecology soil sampling, and simple healthy yard habits.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Historic Soil Plumes Are Completely Manageable in Daily Life</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Keep soil plumes in practical perspective. Historical copper smelters in Ruston (Tacoma) and Northeast Everett operated for decades during the 20th century, carrying microscopic trace particles of arsenic and lead downwind into topsoil across parts of King and Snohomish counties. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        However, for 95 percent of homeowners, this is completely manageable. Any remaining contamination is strictly confined to the top 0 to 6 inches of unpaved, bare dirt. Maintaining healthy lawn grass, using raised beds with fresh soil for vegetable gardens, wiping muddy pet paws, and taking off shoes at the door completely eliminate ingestion risk. In addition, Washington State offers free soil sampling and full soil replacement for eligible residential yards.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The Two Regional Asarco Smelter Sites</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      During the late 19th and 20th centuries, two major copper smelters operated in Western Washington, releasing airborne particles through high smokestacks that settled over surrounding soils.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">1894 to 1912 Era</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Everett Smelter (Snohomish County)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Primary Affected Footprint</span>
          <p class="text-body">A tightly contained 1.1-square-mile residential and industrial area in Northeast Everett. The Department of Ecology operates an active residential soil cleanup program within this zone.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">1890 to 1986 Era</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Tacoma Smelter (King &amp; Pierce Counties)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="calc-field-group">
          <span class="calc-field-label">Primary Affected Footprint</span>
          <p class="text-body">A massive 1,000-square-mile airborne plume footprint extending north over Vashon-Maury Island, West Seattle, Des Moines, and Federal Way due to prevailing southerly winds.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

